# cli.py
This module provides the command-line entry point for generating ledger reports. It defines the `report` command and its options, reads and parses ledger data, optionally applies categorisation rules, formats monthly income, spending, net totals, and top expense categories, and converts domain errors into a user-facing message with a nonzero exit status.

## b1, b2
Set up the `ledger` command-line module, importing argument parsing, process-exit support, filesystem paths, ledger-specific errors, transaction parsing, report calculations and formatting, and optional rule-based categorisation.

## build_parser
Create and return the command-line parser with program name `ledger` and a required `report` subcommand. Require a positional `ledger_file` converted to a `Path`, accept an optional `--rules` path defaulting to `None`, and accept `--top` as an integer count defaulting to `5`.

## run_report
Read all lines from `ledger_file` and turn them into transactions with `parse_lines`. If `rules_file` is supplied, read and load its rules, then categorise the transactions before reporting. Produce a text report with a fixed header, one row per monthly total showing formatted income, outgoings, and net amounts, followed by a `top categories` section containing up to the requested number of categories. Return the complete report as newline-separated text; file, parsing, rule, and reporting errors are allowed to propagate to the caller as `LedgerError` failures.

## main
Parse either the supplied `argv` list or the process command line, run the selected report, print its text to standard output, and return `0` on success. Catch `LedgerError`, print an `error: ...` diagnostic to standard error, and return `1` so command-line failures are reflected in the process status.

## test
unit tests

## b6
When the module is executed directly, invoke `main()` and terminate the process with the exit code it returns; importing the module does not run the command.
